from common import *


def story():
    st = [Paragraph("Mic Placement", title),
          Paragraph("Where each mic goes, how far, and aimed at what, for every mic'd source in the channel map. Distances are starting points; move the mic before you reach for EQ. Room dimensions aren't needed for most of this, since placement is about distance from the source. The places where your room sizes would change the advice are marked \"room-dependent\" and listed at the end.", sub)]
    st += sec("Rules that cover everything", [
        "<b>Move the mic, not the EQ.</b> An inch at a guitar grille or two inches on a vocal changes more than any knob on the console. Set placement with headphones on and someone playing, then set trim, then EQ.",
        "<b>Closer = more bass, less room.</b> Every directional mic you own (SM57, SM58, the knockoffs, Peavey, Worker Bee in cardioid) gets boomier as it gets closer, called proximity effect. Use it on purpose for warmth; back off when a source gets muddy.",
        "<b>Aim the mic's back at what you don't want.</b> Cardioid mics reject sound from directly behind. Point the back of the snare mic at the hi-hat, the back of the vocal mic at the drum kit, and bleed drops without moving anything else.",
        "<b>3:1 rule for two mics on one source.</b> When two mics can hear the same thing (two tom mics, the room pair, a vocal mic near the acoustic guitar mic), keep them at least three times as far from each other as each is from its source, or their sound combines with hollow phase cancellation.",
        "<b>Check in mono.</b> Press MONO in the console's monitor section with the room pair up. If the drums get thinner or the kick disappears, a mic is out of phase: move it a few inches or flip a cable's polarity.",
        "<b>Stands and cables.</b> Boom arm over the heavy tripod leg so it doesn't tip. One loop of cable around the stand. Never let a mic cable hang from the mic's connector."])
    drums = [["Mic / channel", "Where", "Distance", "Aim", "Notes"],
             ["Kick (ch 1)", "Inside the kick through the port hole if the front head has one; otherwise 2-4 in. outside the front head, off-center", "Inside: 3-6 in. from the beater head. Outside: 2-4 in.", "At the beater spot for click; angled toward the shell for more boom", "More click = closer to the beater. Take the pillow out of the drum before you blame the mic. Room-dependent: no."],
             ["Snare (ch 2)", "Top head, over the rim, between the hi-hat and the first rack tom", "1-2 in. above the rim, pointed at the center", "Capsule aimed at the center of the head, body tilted so its back faces the hi-hat", "If the snare sounds thin, lower the angle; if too much hi-hat, rotate the back of the mic toward it. Stand on the drummer's left."],
             ["Rack tom (ch 3)", "Over the top rim, away from the cymbal above it", "1-2 in. above the head, at the rim", "At the center of the head", "SM58-style: pull the grille basket off for a closer, punchier position if it's a true 58 clone."],
             ["Floor tom (ch 4)", "Over the top rim on the side away from the ride", "1-2 in. above the head", "At the center", "Same as the rack tom. Check the 3:1 rule between the two tom mics."],
             ["Room L / R (ch 5-6)", "Room-dependent. Default: a spaced pair, 6-10 ft in front of the kit, 4-6 ft apart, at 5-6 ft high, equidistant from the snare", "6-10 ft from the kit", "Both at the snare", "Measure from the snare to each mic with a tape and make them equal: that keeps the snare centered. The further back, the more room sound; in a small room, 6 ft is plenty. See the room notes."],
             ["Turner 95D as a mono crush mic (optional)", "Low, in front of the kick, or out in the room at chest height", "3-6 ft", "At the kit", "Into the 76-KT, all buttons in. Blend under the close mics. Its narrow range is the point."]]
    st += [KeepTogether([Paragraph("Drums", h), tbl(drums, [1.05 * inch, 1.9 * inch, 1.1 * inch, 1.3 * inch, 2.15 * inch])])]
    amps = [["Mic / channel", "Where", "Distance", "Aim", "Notes"],
            ["Magnatone, SM57 (ch 8)", "On the speaker cone, halfway between the dust cap (center) and the edge", "1-2 in. off the grille", "Straight at the cone", "Toward the center = brighter and more bite; toward the edge = warmer. Off-axis (angled) = smoother top. The Magnatone's vibrato is in the amp, so the mic hears it; no need to add it later."],
            ["Marshall MG250DFX or Orange, SM58-style (ch 9)", "Same as above", "1-2 in.", "At the cone, slightly off-axis", "SM58-style mics are darker than a 57; start nearer the dust cap. For the 2x12 Marshall, pick the speaker that sounds best and mic only that one."],
            ["Bass amp, if mic'd instead of DI (ch 7)", "On the cone, toward the edge", "2-4 in.", "At the cone", "Combine with the DI for the best of both: mic for grit, DI for low end. Check mono for phase between them."],
            ["Blending two amps on group 6", "Both mics as above", "", "", "Both amps share group 6, so set each mic first, then balance on the console faders. Keep the two amps a few feet apart so each mic hears mostly its own."]]
    st += [KeepTogether([Paragraph("Amps", h), tbl(amps, [1.25 * inch, 1.7 * inch, 0.8 * inch, 1.1 * inch, 2.65 * inch])])]
    vox = [["Mic / channel", "Where", "Distance", "Aim", "Notes"],
           ["Lead vocal, Worker Bee (ch 10)", "On a boom in front of the singer, capsule at mouth height or slightly above, with a pop filter", "6-10 in. from the mouth, pop filter at 3-4 in.", "At the mouth, singer slightly off to one side of the capsule's center", "Closer for intimacy and more low end; back off for a shouted take. Hang a blanket or put the singer's back to the room's soft side: a condenser hears the whole room. LO CUT on at the console."],
           ["Vocal 2, Peavey (ch 15)", "Handheld or on a stand", "1-3 in. (dynamic mics like it close)", "At the mouth, slightly off-axis to tame plosives", "Dynamic mics reject the room, so they can sit near the drum kit when the band tracks together. Point its back at the kit."],
           ["Vocal 3, Turner 95D (ch 16)", "On a stand", "2-6 in.", "At the mouth", "Lo-fi by nature. Closer makes it fuller; it has no low end to over-emphasize. Sort its impedance first (Setup Map)."],
           ["Trumpet, SM58 (ch 14)", "In front of the bell, off-center", "12-24 in. from the bell", "Slightly off the bell's axis, not straight down it", "Loudest source in the room. Straight into the bell is harsh and clips; off-axis and back a foot is round. The console trim will be low."],
           ["Acoustic guitar, Worker Bee (ch 10)", "Where the neck meets the body", "8-12 in.", "At the 12th fret, angled slightly toward the sound hole", "Never straight at the sound hole: that's all boom. For strummed parts pull back to 14-18 in."]]
    st += [KeepTogether([Paragraph("Vocals, horn and acoustic", h), tbl(vox, [1.25 * inch, 1.7 * inch, 1.0 * inch, 1.3 * inch, 2.25 * inch])])]
    st += sec("The band tracking together: bleed control", [
        "Order of volume in the room: drums, then amps, then trumpet, then voices. Put the loudest things farthest from the vocal mics, and point every cardioid mic's back at the kit.",
        "Guitar amps: face them away from the drums and away from each other, or put a blanket or a sofa cushion over the back and sides of an open-back combo. Even a chair with a coat on it between amp and kit helps.",
        "Vocals during a band take go on the dynamics (Peavey, SM58), not the Worker Bee: a condenser will record the drums as much as the voice. Overdub the lead vocal with the Worker Bee afterward.",
        "Bass through the DI needs no isolation at all; that's the main reason to use it for live takes.",
        "The room mics will hear everything. That's fine: in a small room they become \"band mics\" and glue the take. Check them in mono before committing."])
    st += sec("Room-dependent decisions (what your measurements would change)", [
        "<b>Where the kit sits.</b> Not in the exact center of the room and not against a wall; about a third of the way along the longer dimension, facing the long way, is the usual best spot. In a very small room, a corner-ish placement with the kick firing diagonally gives the room mics the most distance.",
        "<b>Room mic distance and height.</b> The defaults above assume a room of roughly 10-14 ft on its longer side. Smaller: bring the pair to 4-6 ft and lower, or use one mono room mic. Bigger: push back to 10-12 ft and raise to 6-7 ft. The ceiling height sets the maximum useful mic height: keep the capsules at least 18 in. below the ceiling.",
        "<b>Whether the room is live or dead.</b> Carpet and a low ceiling (like the control room in your photos) is dead: room mics will sound small, and the Turner crush mic becomes the better room sound. Hard floors and bare walls are live: room mics shine, but the vocal needs a blanket behind the singer.",
        "<b>Control room.</b> For mixing on the DIY monitors, the speakers should sit on the long wall's centerline with your head and the two speakers forming an equal triangle, and the first reflection points on the side walls (where a mirror held flat against the wall shows a speaker from your seat) get an absorber. The room's size sets the speaker spacing; with the console desk at about 4 ft wide, the clamp-mounted speakers land roughly 3.5-4 ft apart, so sit about 3.5-4 ft back.",
        "<b>What to send me:</b> length, width and ceiling height of the live room and the control room, and which walls are hard or soft. With those, the room-mic positions, the kit spot and the monitor triangle above become exact numbers instead of ranges."])
    return st
